{{/* The aside is one thing with two halves, and this file holds what says so:
     the button that opens it and the strip that picks the half. Both halves
     are the assistant carrying on by itself, without anybody asking again,
     which is why they stand together and why the aside is called Watching: a
     steered coder is watched while it works, a trigger is waited for until it
     fires.

     They are two tabs and not two sections under each other, because under
     each other the second one is below the fold on every screen small enough
     to matter, and a half nobody scrolls to is a half nobody uses. Side by
     side both are one click away and each starts at the top.

     A count is the one number that still moves: open jobs on the first tab,
     triggers that still fire on the second, the two together on the button,
     because a phone sees nothing of the aside but that button. All three are
     the one badge the cockpit paints a count with, `.dc-steer-badge`, out of
     the flow, so a number that comes and goes moves nothing and the row stands
     still while jobs open and close. Only where it hangs differs: the corner
     of the button's glyph, and on a tab the end of the label, which is the
     anchor because it is the one thing that ends where the word ends. There it
     stands raised, its bottom edge on the word's middle line, so it covers the
     upper half of the ink the way a corner badge covers the top of its glyph,
     and reads as a note at the word instead of a second word beside it. Raised
     it still ends inside the tab's own padding, so it leaves the row's height
     and the scroller's edge alone. The tab itself is none: it is half the
     aside wide with its label centered, so every corner of it stands a
     different distance from each of the two words and the first one lands on
     the seam between the halves. A count still hides at zero, which now spares
     an empty circle and moves nothing either way.

     Nothing else stands in this row. What makes a trigger belongs to the
     triggers, so it stands at the head of that tab and is gone with it, which
     is what a control beside two tabs could never say.

     The two halves are Bootstrap's own `nav-justified`, `flex-basis: 0` plus
     `flex-grow: 1` on every link, so they are equal by the row's width and
     never by their text; `nav-fill` divides what the text leaves and would
     hand the longer name the larger half, which is the crooked strip this
     replaces. They carry `justify-content-center` because Tabler makes a
     `.nav-link` a flex box, so `nav-justified`'s own `text-align` reaches
     nothing, and it is the class Tabler itself puts on a filled nav. There is
     no gap between them: `nav-bordered` takes the horizontal padding off a
     link, so the active half's 2px border runs to the middle of the row and
     says where the half ends, and a gap would only notch that line. */}}
{{define "assistant_watching_button.gohtml"}}
<button type="button" class="btn btn-icon btn-ghost-secondary flex-shrink-0 position-relative d-xl-none" data-bs-toggle="offcanvas" data-bs-target="#assistant-aside"
        aria-label="Watching" title="Watching" data-assistant-watching-button>
  <i class="ti ti-eye" aria-hidden="true"></i>
  <dc-steer-badge class="dc-steer-badge{{if not .WatchingOpen}} d-none{{end}}" count="jobs triggers" aria-hidden="true">{{.WatchingOpen}}</dc-steer-badge>
</button>
{{end}}

{{define "assistant_watching_tabs.gohtml"}}
<div class="nav nav-bordered nav-justified mb-2" role="tablist">
  <button type="button" class="nav-link justify-content-center active" id="assistant-coders-tab" data-bs-toggle="tab" data-bs-target="#assistant-coders"
          role="tab" aria-controls="assistant-coders" aria-selected="true" data-assistant-coders-tab>
    <i class="ti ti-steering-wheel me-1" aria-hidden="true"></i><span class="position-relative" data-assistant-tab-label>Steered coders<dc-steer-badge class="dc-steer-badge{{if not .JobsOpen}} d-none{{end}}" count="jobs" aria-hidden="true">{{.JobsOpen}}</dc-steer-badge></span>
  </button>
  <button type="button" class="nav-link justify-content-center" id="assistant-triggers-tab" data-bs-toggle="tab" data-bs-target="#assistant-triggers"
          role="tab" aria-controls="assistant-triggers" aria-selected="false" data-assistant-triggers-tab>
    <i class="ti ti-bolt me-1" aria-hidden="true"></i><span class="position-relative" data-assistant-tab-label>Triggers<dc-steer-badge class="dc-steer-badge{{if not .Triggers.Open}} d-none{{end}}" count="triggers" aria-hidden="true">{{.Triggers.Open}}</dc-steer-badge></span>
  </button>
</div>
{{end}}

{{/* The aside of an assistant's page, the owner's and a guest's of a link.
     A guest reads both halves and makes nothing in them. */}}
{{define "assistant_aside.gohtml"}}
{{/* The aside holds what the assistant carries on by itself: the coders
     it steers and the triggers it waits for. Two tabs of equal
     standing, built alike, because they are one thing to the reader,
     work that goes on without being asked again. Beside the conversation
     from xl up, below xl the sheet the head's eye opens. The memory sheet
     is the layout's, opened from the list column's head. */}}
<aside class="offcanvas-xl offcanvas-end dc-assistant-aside" tabindex="-1" id="assistant-aside" aria-label="Watching" data-assistant-aside>
  {{/* The same head the list column and the thread wear, so all three end
       on one line; see the memory sheet in ctx_fragments.gohtml. It is
       the sheet's head alone: from xl up the aside stands beside the
       thread under the page's own head, there is nothing to close and
       the list starts at once. Off by the width (`d-xl-none`, the
       breakpoint of `offcanvas-xl`), never by the offcanvas state, the
       element is the same one in both sizes. */}}
  <div class="dc-ctx-head d-xl-none">
    <h2 class="dc-ctx-title">Watching</h2>
    <button type="button" class="btn-close" data-bs-dismiss="offcanvas" data-bs-target="#assistant-aside" aria-label="Close"></button>
  </div>
  {{/* Two tabs, one list each, and the strip stands outside the body a
       list swaps, so a refresh leaves the chosen half and every unfolded
       row exactly as they were. */}}
  <div class="offcanvas-body dc-assistant-aside-body">
    {{template "assistant_watching_tabs.gohtml" .}}
    <div class="tab-content">
      <dc-assistant-list class="tab-pane active" id="assistant-coders" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="assistant-coders-tab" list-url="{{.JobsListURL}}" data-assistant-jobs-list>
        {{template "assistant_jobs_list.gohtml" .}}
      </dc-assistant-list>
      <dc-assistant-list class="tab-pane" id="assistant-triggers" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="assistant-triggers-tab" list-url="{{.TriggersListURL}}" data-assistant-triggers-list>
        {{/* What makes a trigger stands at the head of the triggers, the
             plus with the word beside it. It is the tab pane's own child,
             so the tab carries it and nothing switches a visibility by
             hand, and it stands outside the body the list swaps, so an
             event of the assistant leaves it where it is. The empty state
             below says what a trigger is for and offers nothing, because
             this button is already the line above it.

             It runs the full width and carries no colour of its own: a
             coloured block beside nothing reads as a thing dropped in,
             while the full width closes the strip off above the list. Its
             weight is the aside's own, `btn btn-sm`, what a trigger row's
             Open coder and Change wear one level down, and colour is left
             to the one action that destroys something. */}}
        {{if not .Access.Guest}}<a href="{{.TriggersNewURL}}" class="btn btn-sm w-100 mb-2" data-assistant-trigger-new>
          <i class="ti ti-plus me-1" aria-hidden="true"></i>New trigger
        </a>{{end}}
        {{template "assistant_triggers_list.gohtml" .Triggers}}
      </dc-assistant-list>
    </div>
  </div>
</aside>
{{end}}
